Three points define a certain PV curve.  
Point 1 has P=1 bar, V=1 cubic meter. 
Point 2 has 5 bars, V=1 cubic meter. 
Point 3 has P=1 bar, V=5 cubic meters.  
Between Point 2 and Point 3, PV=constant.  
The cycle is performed on 1 kg of air.  

What is the heat input into the cycle? 

Thoughts expand_moreTo find the total heat input into the cycle, 
we analyze each leg of the three-step cycle on the $PV$ diagram 
using the First Law of Thermodynamics1 and the Ideal Gas Law23.1. 

I.  Cycle Overview & Process Identification

Converting pressure to standard units ($1\text{ bar} = 10^5\text{ Pa}$):
Point 1: $P_1 = 1\text{ bar} = 10^5\text{ Pa}$, $V_1 = 1\text{ m}^3$
Point 2: $P_2 = 5\text{ bar} = 5 \times 10^5\text{ Pa}$, $V_2 = 1\text{ m}^3$
Point 3: $P_3 = 1\text{ bar} = 10^5\text{ Pa}$, $V_3 = 5\text{ m}^3$

The cycle consists of three distinct processes:

1. Process 1 $\rightarrow$ 2 (Constant Volume / Isochoric Heating): 
Volume remains fixed at $V = 1\text{ m}^3$ while pressure increases 
from $1\text{ bar}$ to $5\text{ bar}$.

2. Process 2 $\rightarrow$ 3 (Isothermal Expansion): 
Since $P_2 V_2 = P_3 V_3 = 5\text{ bar}\cdot\text{m}^3$, the path 
$PV = \text{constant}$ represents an isothermal (constant temperature) 
expansion

3. Process 3 $\rightarrow$ 1 (Constant Pressure / Isobaric Compression): 
Pressure remains fixed at $P = 1\text{ bar}$ while volume decreases 
from $5\text{ m}^3$ to $1\text{ m}^3$.

II. Calculating Heat Transfer for Each Process

For air modeled as an ideal diatomic gas, the adiabatic index is 
$\gamma = 1.4$ (so $\gamma - 1 = 0.4$)

4.Process 1 $\rightarrow$ 2 (Isochoric Heating)Work done ($W_{12}$): 

Since volume is constant, $W_{12} = 0\text{ J}$.Internal energy change 

($\Delta U_{12}$): $$\Delta U_{12} = \frac{P_2 V_2 - P_1 V_1}{\gamma - 1} = \frac{(5 \times 10^5)(1) - (1 \times 10^5)(1)}{0.4} = \frac{4 \times 10^5}{0.4} = 1,000,000\text{ J} = 1000\text{ kJ}$$

Heat added ($Q_{12}$): $$Q_{12} = \Delta U_{12} + W_{12} = \mathbf{1000\text{ kJ}} \quad (\text{Heat Input})$$

Process 2 $\rightarrow$ 3 (Isothermal Expansion)

Internal energy change ($\Delta U_{23}$): 

Temperature is constant, so $\Delta U_{23} = 0\text{ J}$.

Work done ($W_{23}$): 

$$W_{23} = P_2 V_2 \ln\left(\frac{V_3}{V_2}\right) = (5 \times 10^5\text{ J}) \times \ln(5) \approx 5 \times 10^5 \times 1.60944 \approx 804,719\text{ J} = 804.72\text{ kJ}$$

Heat added ($Q_{23}$): $$Q_{23} = \Delta U_{23} + W_{23} = \mathbf{804.72\text{ kJ}} \quad (\text{Heat Input})$$

Process 3 $\rightarrow$ 1 (Isobaric Compression)

Work done ($W_{31}$): $W_{31} = P_1(V_1 - V_3) = 10^5 \times (1 - 5) = -400,000\text{ J} = -400\text{ kJ}$

Internal energy change ($\Delta U_{31}$): $\Delta U_{31} = \frac{P_1 V_1 - P_3 V_3}{\gamma - 1} = \frac{-4 \times 10^5}{0.4} = -1000\text{ kJ}$

Heat released ($Q_{31}$): $Q_{31} = \Delta U_{31} + W_{31} = -1400\text{ kJ}$ ($\text{Heat Rejected / Output}$)

3. Total Heat Input

Heat enters the cycle during processes 1 $\rightarrow$ 2 and 2 $\rightarrow$ 3 (where $Q > 0$):

$$Q_{\text{in}} = Q_{12} + Q_{23} = 1000\text{ kJ} + 804.72\text{ kJ} = \mathbf{1804.72\text{ kJ}} \quad (\text{or } \mathbf{1.805\text{ MJ}})$$
